 # monday gotchas
 
-The one dated list of what the monday API cannot say about itself (A§10.2). Skills and references link to an entry by its id and never restate it. Every entry comes from the live runs of 2026-09-10 and 2026-09-11 in the test workspace, the three recheck passes, the native structure run of 2026-09-11, and the probes and recapture of 2026-09-12. Nothing here comes from a learn file, from documentation alone, or from memory.
+The one dated list of what the monday API cannot say about itself (A§10.2). Skills and references link to an entry by its id and never restate it. Every entry comes from the live runs of 2026-09-10 and 2026-09-11 in the test workspace, the three recheck passes, the native structure run of 2026-09-11, the probes and recapture of 2026-09-12, and the first two demonstration builds of 2026-10-22 (section 13). Nothing here comes from a learn file, from documentation alone, or from memory.
 
 **How to read an entry.** Every entry carries the same eight facts: the claim; the surface (a tool by base name, or "raw" for a GraphQL document sent through `all_api_read`, `all_api_write`, `all_monday_api` or `execute_code`); the ring the probe observed; what to do instead; the evidence with its date; the probe that re-tests it, as a recipe id in `probe-catalog.md` (`monday-drift-check`), marked read-only or sandbox write; its impact; and its status. High-impact entries carry the full block; the rest carry the same eight facts on one line under their heading. Ids never renumber; a retired entry keeps its id for one release.
 
@@ -53,7 +53,7 @@ Claim: `boards(workspace_ids, state: all)` returns ordinary boards, `document` d
 ## 3. Boards and folders (G-12 to G-20)
 
 ## G-12 · create_board ignores folderId unless creationPrompt is sent
-Claim: without `creationPrompt`, five tool-created boards (plain, multi-level flag, dataset flag, private kind) passed `folderId` and landed at the workspace root; with a prompt the board went into the folder; raw `create_board(folder_id)` placed correctly with or without `empty: true`. Surface: `create_board`; raw `create_board`. Ring: release_candidate 2026-10. Do instead: read `board_folder_id` after every tool-created board and `move_object` when it is null, or create raw with `folder_id` and `empty: true` (A§12.1 C2). Evidence: executed with read-back of `board_folder_id` and the folder's children, 2026-09-11 (boards-folders-hierarchy; recheck-1 agrees; the cause is inferred, not proven). Probe: sandbox write, recipe P-G12 in probe-catalog.md. Impact: medium. Status: confirmed.
+Claim: without `creationPrompt`, five tool-created boards (plain, multi-level flag, dataset flag, private kind) passed `folderId` and landed at the workspace root; with a prompt the board went into the folder; raw `create_board(folder_id)` placed correctly with or without `empty: true`. Surface: `create_board`; raw `create_board`. Ring: release_candidate 2026-10. Do instead: read `board_folder_id` after every tool-created board and `move_object` when it is null, or create raw with `folder_id` and `empty: true` (A§12.1 C2). Evidence: executed with read-back of `board_folder_id` and the folder's children, 2026-09-11 (boards-folders-hierarchy; recheck-1 agrees; the cause is inferred, not proven); seen again in a demonstration build, 2026-10-22, where the tool's first board landed at the root and raw `folder_id` placed the next. Probe: sandbox write, recipe P-G12 in probe-catalog.md. Impact: medium. Status: confirmed.
 ## G-13 · move_object places a board in a folder
 Claim: `move_object(objectType: Board, id, parentFolderId)` answered "Board position updated" and the board read back inside the folder in both `board_folder_id` and the folder's children; the same call can also change workspace and account product (G-106). Surface: `move_object`. Ring: release_candidate 2026-10. Do instead: use it as the placement fix after creation and read the result back. Evidence: executed with read-back, 2026-09-11 (boards-folders-hierarchy; recheck-1 agrees). Probe: sandbox write, recipe P-G13 in probe-catalog.md. Impact: low. Status: confirmed.
 ## G-14 · Raw create_board adds a sample item unless empty: true
@@ -392,6 +392,21 @@ Claim: a duplicate of a native keeps every column-level fingerprint and differs
 ## G-108 · The board permissions field reads back at board level, and nothing below it is readable
 Claim: `permissions` returned `"everyone"` on a native CRM board and on an ordinary board through a plain `boards(ids)` read; no field read exposed column restrictions or item-level or subitem-level permissions; no changed level was written, so the read is proved to exist and return a value, not to reflect a build's change. Surface: raw `boards { permissions }`. Ring: release_candidate 2026-10. Do instead: prove a board-level permission through the field, marked [doc, P-18]; record everything below it `not-checkable` and put it on the by-eye list, so a built permission is either proved or honestly unproved and never reported as verified (`read-back.md` permissions row, A§10.4); the changed-level half is sandbox scenario T1. Evidence: executed read-only, 2026-09-12 (probes P-18, two boards). Probe: read-only today, recipe P-G108 in probe-catalog.md; the write half runs in the sandbox only. Impact: medium. Status: observed.
 
+## 13. Found by the demonstration builds (G-150 onward)
+
+The live findings of the first two demonstration builds, 2026-10-22, numbered from 150 to stay clear of the four bands after G-108 that A§10.2 keeps for the AI kinds. G-12 holds a third finding of the same builds, the board placed outside its folder.
+
+## G-150 · A one-way relation column can be made two-way in place
+Claim: `update_column` on a one-way `board_relation` with `allowCreateReflectionColumn: true` and `boardIds` resent (G-29) created the matching relation column on the other board and filled it from the links already stored, so every linked item showed its link at once; monday named the new column "link to" and the board's name. Surface: `update_column`. Ring: release_candidate 2026-10. Do instead: create a relation two-way from the start; to make a one-way relation two-way, send this update, rename the new column to the planned name and read both boards back. Evidence: executed with read-back of both boards, 2026-10-22 (a demonstration build, one sample). Probe: sandbox write, recipe P-G150 in probe-catalog.md. Impact: medium. Status: observed.
+## G-151 · Status label ids come back as numbers, and 0 is one of them
+Claim: the labels of a status or dropdown column read back as a list of objects whose `id` is a JSON number, and the ids include 0, so a check that holds every id to one string spelling refuses a sound read. Surface: raw `columns { settings }`, `get_board_info`. Ring: release_candidate 2026-10. Do instead: read label ids as label numbers, never as monday ids: keep them as read, 0 included (G-22 for what the numbers mean). Evidence: executed with read-back, 2026-10-22 (a demonstration build, every label column it read). Probe: read-only, recipe P-G151 in probe-catalog.md. Impact: medium. Status: observed.
+## G-152 · A lower-case sort direction is stored as ASC
+Claim: `create_view_table` sent `direction: "desc"`, the form the tool documents, stored the sort as `ASC`; the same view updated with `DESC` stored it descending. Surface: `create_view_table`, `update_view_table`. Ring: release_candidate 2026-10. Do instead: send `ASC` and `DESC` exactly as written here and read the stored sort back; a lower-case `asc` looks right only because `ASC` is what it stores anyway. Evidence: executed with read-back, 2026-10-22 (a demonstration build, one view, updated once). Probe: sandbox write, recipe P-G152 in probe-catalog.md. Impact: medium. Status: observed.
+## G-153 · create_automation can pick another product's notify block, and map a field to the wrong column
+Claim: asked to notify the user, `create_automation` chose a notify block of Microsoft Teams in two builds, and in one stored nothing; with monday's own "Notify someone" action named, it stored the right trigger, action and recipient but mapped the message's placeholder for a dropdown column to a status column of another name. Surface: `create_automation`, `list_automations`. Ring: release_candidate 2026-10. Do instead: name monday's own "Notify someone" action, the recipient and each column the message reads, by name and type; read back every field the message reads, not only the trigger, recipient and offset; an automation stored wrong departs to a UI step and is never patched with a second one (G-65, G-66). Evidence: executed with read-back, 2026-10-22 (two demonstration builds). Probe: sandbox write, recipe P-G153 in probe-catalog.md. Impact: medium. Status: observed.
+## G-154 · An automation's time of day is stored in the account's time zone
+Claim: a date trigger planned for 7:00 stored `hour 7, minute 0` with a day offset and the account's own time zone, while a time written into an item's date is stored in UTC (G-43). Surface: `create_automation`, `list_automations`. Ring: release_candidate 2026-10. Do instead: give an automation's time of day in the account's local time and check the time zone in the read; never convert it to UTC first. Evidence: executed with read-back, 2026-10-22 (a demonstration build, one sample). Probe: sandbox write, recipe P-G154 in probe-catalog.md. Impact: low. Status: observed.
+
 ## Never say
 
 A claims block, not a gotcha: the per-entry fields above do not apply to it. `demo-script` reads it at generation (A§3.5) and `deferred/shared/house-style.md` (v2, not shipped) links its DON'T list here; `call-intake` uses it when it writes the deferred-answer register. Each row is a claims rule tied to the probe evidence behind it, with the sentence that replaces the claim. Rows 8 to 12 are the five the calibration added (`REAL-CALL-LESSONS.md` C51); rows 13 to 15 are its three companion rules that name this file (C46, C92, C101).
